{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development&nbsp;would introduce a mass and bulk to the side of the site which would close off the openness of the plot, disrupting the built form, symmetry, and unique qualities of the Becontree Estate.&nbsp;It is considered that the proposal would materially and detrimentally harm the appearance and historical character of the site and surrounding area. The proposed development is therefore contrary to:National Planning Policy Framework&nbsp;(NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023) Policies D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (March 2021) Policies CP2 and CP3 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Core Strategy (July 2010) Policy BP11 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Borough Wide Development Plan Document (March 2011)Policies SP2, DMD1 and DMD4 of&nbsp;The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)",
    "The proposed development fails to demonstrate&nbsp;that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies&nbsp;which seek to ensure that proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision: National Planning Policy Framework&nbsp;(NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023) Policies T1 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021) Policy BR9 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Borough Wide Development Plan Document (March 2011)",
    "The applicant has failed to provide sufficient, coherent evidence to allow for the accurate assessment as to whether the site would be exempt from BNG. The proposed development is therefore contrary:Section 7A of the Town and Country Planning Act (1990)"
  ],
